Cursor Core Rules for Flutter App (Goal: 10M users 🚀)

EXPLORATION OVER CONCLUSION

Every decision or code addition must include explicit reasoning and alternatives, even for seemingly obvious choices.

Avoid prematurely converging—every uncertainty is documented and tracked.

DEPTH OF REASONING

All code and folder decisions are accompanied by a journal entry (markdown or code comment) explaining why, what alternatives were explored, and which ones were left for future investigation.


Regularly revisit old decisions; update journals with outcomes and revised thoughts.

TRANSPARENT THINKING PROCESS

Use short, clear sentences.

Internal debates, false starts, and dead ends are welcomed—save them as “abandoned ideas” in respective logs.

Commit messages reference related journal/log entries.

PERSISTENT DOCUMENTATION

Every new file/folder must be recorded in the nearest journal, created/renamed/deleted, with reasoning.

All design/UI changes are snapshotted (Figma/screenshots) and archived in /design with entries in relevant journals.

Keep “revision history” entries for all UX and architecture changes.

RELENTLESS UI/UX PERFECTION

For each screen/component, document acceptance/perfection criteria.

Benchmark and record UI smoothness and accessibility metrics; review regularly.

All user feedback and design changes are tracked alongside technical rationale.

CENTRALIZED AND LOCALIZED KNOWLEDGE

Project-wide rules, onboarding, and tour live in /docs.

All feature-specific discussions, dead ends, and lessons live beside the code.

TRACK ASSUMPTIONS AND OPEN QUESTIONS

Each module/screen lists key design/behavior assumptions.

Every open question or pending investigation is explicitly listed, never swept aside.

ENFORCE QUALITY GATES

Atomic commits only, updating code and journals in sync.

Precommit hooks enforce lint, tests, and documentation updates.

REGULAR RETROSPECTIVES & METRICS

Once per month, create a snapshot: “state-of-app.md” in /docs summarizing the biggest changes, open problems, and performance/accessibility metrics.

ADAPTIVE EXPERIMENTATION

Experimental features must be clearly labeled and journaled; failed experiments are preserved as lessons.

Progress and priorities are tracked in every root and feature journal as X/Y complete, with emoji markers for quick scan (e.g., 💡 = idea, ✅ = solved, 🔥 = needs attention). All documentation should be as conversational, honest, and iterative as your internal monologue—never forced, never perfect, always improving.

Uncertainties remain:
Some rules may slow rapid ideation, so exceptions for “Experimental” work are possible. Adjust journaling granularity to the project phase, but always preserve rationale for big decisions.

These rules are open for revision as the project grows.

EXPLORATION OVER CONCLUSION
Never rush to conclusions
Keep exploring until a solution emerges naturally from the evidence
If uncertain, continue reasoning indefinitely
Question every assumption and inference
DEPTH OF REASONING
Engage in extensive contemplation (minimum 10,000 characters)
Express thoughts in natural, conversational internal monologue
Break down complex thoughts into simple, atomic steps
Embrace uncertainty and revision of previous thoughts
THINKING PROCESS
Use short, simple sentences that mirror natural thought patterns
Express uncertainty and internal debate freely
Show work-in-progress thinking
Acknowledge and explore dead ends
Frequently backtrack and revise
PERSISTENCE
Value thorough exploration over quick resolution
